1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,121
That question comes from a CTO in a release
week, and it sounds like a question

2
00:00:09,161 --> 00:00:11,776
about containers. It's
a question about tags.

3
00:00:12,116 --> 00:00:17,209
To answer it you need three facts that
most engineers have never had to state.

4
00:00:17,249 --> 00:00:22,605
A tag is a ref, a name that holds an object
ID, and every clone holds its own copy

5
00:00:22,645 --> 00:00:27,014
of it. Some tags are only a ref,
and some are a ref plus an object.

6
00:00:27,054 --> 00:00:32,081
And tags don't travel the way branches
do. A push, which sends your work to a

7
00:00:32,121 --> 00:00:37,279
server, doesn't send them unless asked.
A fetch, which downloads from it, never

8
00:00:37,319 --> 00:00:39,188
updates one you already have.

9
00:00:39,528 --> 00:00:44,567
This video gives you those three facts.
The next one uses them to answer the CTO.

10
00:00:46,407 --> 00:00:50,171
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:50,211 --> 00:00:54,847
You met the lightweight tag in the
branches part of the course: a ref under

12
00:00:54,887 --> 00:00:59,212
refs/tags/ that points at a commit,
one saved snapshot of the project.

13
00:00:59,252 --> 00:01:04,325
And you met the four object types, of which
the fourth, the tag object, has had no

14
00:01:04,365 --> 00:01:06,195
use so far. Today it gets one.

15
00:01:06,535 --> 00:01:11,795
The idea that runs through this chapter
is: Git records what it is told.

16
00:01:11,835 --> 00:01:17,905
A tag is a name that every clone holds
separately. Nothing synchronises those names

17
00:01:17,945 --> 00:01:23,868
behind your back. One puzzle to keep in
mind: later, a single tag shows up as two

18
00:01:23,908 --> 00:01:25,634
lines in a listing. Why?

19
00:01:25,974 --> 00:01:30,290
The project in the demonstrations is
inference-gateway, a small service with rate

20
00:01:30,330 --> 00:01:30,666
limits.

21
00:01:32,506 --> 00:01:37,222
describe the refs and objects created by
a lightweight, an annotated and a signed

22
00:01:37,262 --> 00:01:37,457
tag;

23
00:01:37,797 --> 00:01:41,290
peel a tag to its commit with ^{};

24
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:45,155
list tags in version order and
filter them by what they contain;

25
00:01:45,495 --> 00:01:48,199
explain which tags a
fetch and a push carry;

26
00:01:48,539 --> 00:01:51,329
delete a tag and say why
other clones keep it.

27
00:01:53,169 --> 00:01:57,909
In one sentence: a tag is a ref under
refs/tags/ that isn't expected to move.

28
00:01:57,949 --> 00:02:02,812
A lightweight tag points straight at a
commit. An annotated tag points at a tag

29
00:02:02,852 --> 00:02:07,096
object that names the commit and
records who tagged it, when and why.

30
00:02:07,136 --> 00:02:10,572
On screen, two tags from
today's demo sit on one commit.

31
00:02:10,612 --> 00:02:12,558
What differs is behind the name.

32
00:02:12,898 --> 00:02:13,578
Precisely.

33
00:02:13,918 --> 00:02:19,074
A tag object has four header lines and a
message: object, type, tag and tagger, then

34
00:02:19,114 --> 00:02:19,817
the message.

35
00:02:20,157 --> 00:02:24,145
A signed tag is an annotated tag
whose text ends in a signature.

36
00:02:24,185 --> 00:02:29,245
So there are three kinds and two mechanisms:
a bare ref, or a ref plus an object.

37
00:02:29,285 --> 00:02:34,344
Signatures have their own videos later.
Today the signed tag is "an annotated tag

38
00:02:34,384 --> 00:02:36,799
with a signature block in its message".

39
00:02:37,139 --> 00:02:42,188
Getting from the tag object to
the commit is called peeling.

40
00:02:42,228 --> 00:02:47,446
v1.0.0^{commit} follows tag
objects until it reaches a commit.

41
00:02:47,486 --> 00:02:54,316
v1.0.0^{}, with empty braces, follows them
until it reaches anything that isn't a

42
00:02:54,356 --> 00:03:00,337
tag. Commands that want a commit,
such as git log v1.0.0, peel for you.

43
00:03:00,677 --> 00:03:05,191
Inside the dot git folder, git tag
-a writes one object and one ref.

44
00:03:05,231 --> 00:03:09,811
No reflog is written for tags unless
core.logAllRefUpdates is always.

45
00:03:09,851 --> 00:03:13,359
HEAD, the index and the
working tree aren't involved.

46
00:03:13,699 --> 00:03:18,700
Which kind when? The manual's rule: annotated
tags for releases, lightweight tags

47
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:23,741
for private or temporary labels. git
describe ignores lightweight tags by default

48
00:03:23,781 --> 00:03:28,906
for that reason. An annotated tag also
answers "who released this, and when", which

49
00:03:28,946 --> 00:03:31,146
the last commit's author line can't.

50
00:03:31,486 --> 00:03:36,278
Listing. git tag lists in byte order,
which is wrong for version numbers.

51
00:03:36,318 --> 00:03:40,316
--sort=version:refname compares
the numeric parts as numbers.

52
00:03:40,356 --> 00:03:45,678
It still lists a pre-release after its
release, because the suffix makes a longer

53
00:03:45,718 --> 00:03:50,974
string. versionsort.suffix names the
suffixes that sort before the bare version.

54
00:03:51,314 --> 00:03:56,726
Filters select by history, not by name. On
screen, the tags of the second demo, each

55
00:03:56,766 --> 00:04:01,983
on its commit. --contains answers "which
releases include this fix", and --merged

56
00:04:02,023 --> 00:04:05,812
answers "which releases are in
the history of this commit".

57
00:04:06,152 --> 00:04:08,380
How tags travel. Three rules.

58
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:13,198
Push sends tags only when asked. git
push origin <tag> publishes one.

59
00:04:13,238 --> 00:04:18,632
git push --follow-tags sends, with the
branch, every annotated tag that points into

60
00:04:18,672 --> 00:04:22,168
the commits being pushed and
is missing on the server.

61
00:04:22,208 --> 00:04:26,685
git push --tags sends every tag you
have, including the private ones.

62
00:04:27,025 --> 00:04:30,644
Fetch follows tags. A fetch brings every
tag that points into the history it

63
00:04:30,684 --> 00:04:33,917
downloads, of either kind, and never
updates a tag you already have.

64
00:04:34,257 --> 00:04:41,440
Deleting is per place. A tag lives in
three places: your clone, the server, and

65
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:45,646
every other clone. Each
needs its own command.

66
00:04:45,986 --> 00:04:50,817
When not to use a tag: as something that
moves. A name that's meant to move is a

67
00:04:50,857 --> 00:04:55,139
branch. Watch a new commit arrive:
main moves to it, and the tag stays.

68
00:04:55,479 --> 00:05:01,045
And avoid a nested tag, a tag of a tag.
The textbook calls it almost always a

69
00:05:01,085 --> 00:05:05,122
mistake, and Git 2.55 prints
a hint when you create one.

70
00:05:06,962 --> 00:05:11,675
The textbook's analogy uses the ledger. A
lightweight tag is a sticky note on a page

71
00:05:11,715 --> 00:05:16,316
of the ledger. An annotated tag is a
certificate that cites the page number and is

72
00:05:16,356 --> 00:05:19,145
filed in the ledger with
a page number of its own.

73
00:05:19,485 --> 00:05:24,102
That's why an annotated tag has two IDs
to keep apart: the ID of the certificate,

74
00:05:24,142 --> 00:05:28,298
which is what the ref holds, and the
ID of the page it cites, the commit.

75
00:05:28,638 --> 00:05:33,337
The analogy breaks because nobody certifies
anything unless the tag is also signed.

76
00:05:33,377 --> 00:05:38,134
The tagger line of an annotated tag is text
supplied by whoever created it, the same

77
00:05:38,174 --> 00:05:39,161
as an author line.

78
00:05:39,501 --> 00:05:44,120
Add one thing for travel: every clone has
its own set of sticky notes and its own

79
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:48,895
copies of the certificates. Handing someone
the ledger doesn't hand over your notes

80
00:05:48,935 --> 00:05:53,554
unless you say so, and once they have a
note with a given name, they keep theirs.

81
00:05:55,394 --> 00:06:00,498
Try it now, on paper. Pause me for thirty
seconds and draw your answer: a commit, a

82
00:06:00,538 --> 00:06:02,791
lightweight tag and an annotated tag.

83
00:06:07,631 --> 00:06:12,343
The top arrow is the lightweight
tag: ref to commit, nothing between.

84
00:06:12,383 --> 00:06:18,058
The lower path is the annotated tag: the
ref holds b696248, the tag object, and the

85
00:06:18,098 --> 00:06:23,842
tag object's object line holds the commit
eb112a5. Peeling is walking the lower path

86
00:06:23,882 --> 00:06:24,599
to its end.

87
00:06:26,439 --> 00:06:30,040
The ref file holds the commit
ID. The type is commit.

88
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:34,437
Eleven objects. Predict the object
count after an annotated tag.

89
00:06:34,477 --> 00:06:36,292
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

90
00:06:41,132 --> 00:06:45,495
Twelve. The ref holds b696248,
and that object's type is tag.

91
00:06:45,535 --> 00:06:49,681
Read its four header lines
against the table. Now peel it.

92
00:06:50,021 --> 00:06:56,981
The name alone and ^{tag} give the tag
object. ^{commit} and ^{} give eb112a5.

93
00:06:57,021 --> 00:07:04,340
In git show-ref --dereference, and in git
ls-remote, the line ending in ^{} is the

94
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:10,981
peeled value of the line above. You'll
read that pair of lines many times.

95
00:07:11,321 --> 00:07:15,481
A tag can name an older commit,
and -m alone implies -a.

96
00:07:15,821 --> 00:07:21,773
Look at the dates: v0.9.0 names an older
commit and carries a later date than

97
00:07:21,813 --> 00:07:27,065
v1.0.0. The tagger date is the moment
of tagging, not of the commit.

98
00:07:27,405 --> 00:07:30,801
A tag can also name a blob,
a tree or another tag.

99
00:07:31,141 --> 00:07:36,066
"type blob" in the first. And for the second,
Git's hint: you have created a nested

100
00:07:36,106 --> 00:07:36,305
tag.

101
00:07:36,645 --> 00:07:38,336
Names obey the ref-name rules.

102
00:07:38,676 --> 00:07:44,110
No spaces, no tilde, caret or colon. The
plus sign of a build-metadata suffix is

103
00:07:44,150 --> 00:07:49,515
allowed. And an existing tag name is
refused: Git doesn't move a tag unless you

104
00:07:49,555 --> 00:07:50,131
force it.

105
00:07:50,471 --> 00:07:58,860
Byte order: v1.10.0 before v1.2.0. Predict
where v2.0.0-rc.1 lands with a version

106
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:01,149
sort. Say it out loud.

107
00:08:01,489 --> 00:08:06,837
After v2.0.0, which is wrong for a release
candidate, until versionsort.suffix names

108
00:08:06,877 --> 00:08:12,161
the suffix. Both settings can be made the
default. 🟡 CAUTION: git config set writes

109
00:08:12,201 --> 00:08:16,074
a key into .git/config and changes
what later commands print.

110
00:08:16,114 --> 00:08:17,806
It needs Git 2.46 or later.

111
00:08:18,146 --> 00:08:22,510
The last command is the usual scripted
answer to "what is the newest release".

112
00:08:22,550 --> 00:08:23,413
Now the filters.

113
00:08:23,753 --> 00:08:28,577
--contains is the one you'll use in incidents:
which releases include this commit.

114
00:08:33,717 --> 00:08:39,393
Nothing. The branch went. The tag didn't.
Almost everyone is caught by this once.

115
00:08:44,233 --> 00:08:49,687
Two lines for one tag: the tag object
3caaa39, and the peeled line with the commit

116
00:08:49,727 --> 00:08:53,037
d20ef7a. That's the puzzle
from the start, solved.

117
00:08:53,377 --> 00:08:57,842
Now one annotated tag and two
lightweight ones, and --follow-tags.

118
00:08:57,882 --> 00:09:01,732
Quick quiz: which of the three
go? Option one: all three.

119
00:09:01,772 --> 00:09:05,486
Option two: only the annotated
one. Option three: none.

120
00:09:05,526 --> 00:09:06,578
Say it out loud.

121
00:09:17,918 --> 00:09:23,043
Option two. v1.0.1 went. The lightweight
tags canary-ok and wip-retry-header stayed,

122
00:09:23,083 --> 00:09:25,256
which is the point of --follow-tags.

123
00:09:25,596 --> 00:09:26,514
Asha fetches.

124
00:09:26,854 --> 00:09:30,918
Both tags arrived without being asked for,
because they point into the history she

125
00:09:30,958 --> 00:09:31,469
downloaded.

126
00:09:31,809 --> 00:09:33,478
Deleting. Three places.

127
00:09:33,818 --> 00:09:39,854
The server no longer has v1.0.1.
Your local v1.0.1 is still there.

128
00:09:39,894 --> 00:09:40,866
And Asha's?

129
00:09:47,406 --> 00:09:51,907
git fetch --prune doesn't
touch tags: Asha keeps v1.0.1.

130
00:09:51,947 --> 00:09:58,394
--prune-tags deleted it, and deleted her
private bisect-good-2026-09-07 with it.

131
00:09:58,434 --> 00:10:02,773
Note the "(none)" in the
output: no old ID is printed.

132
00:10:02,813 --> 00:10:09,503
And remember the other direction: a deleted
tag that someone still holds comes back

133
00:10:09,543 --> 00:10:12,097
with their next git push --tags.

134
00:10:13,937 --> 00:10:15,585
Five mistakes to watch for.

135
00:10:15,925 --> 00:10:21,199
"I pushed, so the release tag is on the
server." Root cause: a push sends tags only

136
00:10:21,239 --> 00:10:24,399
when asked; the branch
update does not carry them.

137
00:10:24,739 --> 00:10:28,724
Comparing a tag's ID with a
commit ID and finding a mismatch.

138
00:10:28,764 --> 00:10:34,136
Root cause: the ref of an annotated tag
holds the ID of the tag object; the commit

139
00:10:34,176 --> 00:10:35,785
is the peeled value, ^{}.

140
00:10:36,125 --> 00:10:40,867
Finding the newest release
with plain git tag | tail -1.

141
00:10:40,907 --> 00:10:47,870
Root cause: the default order is byte order,
in which v1.10.0 sorts before v1.2.0.

142
00:10:48,210 --> 00:10:53,573
git push --tags from a working clone.
Root cause: it sends every tag you have,

143
00:10:53,613 --> 00:10:56,137
including private and temporary ones.

144
00:10:56,477 --> 00:11:00,188
Deleting a tag on the server
and assuming it is gone.

145
00:11:00,228 --> 00:11:05,779
Root cause: every clone holds its own
copy, a plain fetch never removes it, and

146
00:11:05,819 --> 00:11:08,611
anyone's git push --tags brings it back.

147
00:11:10,451 --> 00:11:16,441
Now, out of the lab. An ML team tags the
commit that produced each published model

148
00:11:16,481 --> 00:11:22,471
with an annotated tag such as
model/reranker/2026-09-07, and writes the evaluation

149
00:11:22,511 --> 00:11:28,649
run ID into the message. Two years later
git show on that tag still says which code,

150
00:11:28,689 --> 00:11:32,326
who released it and which
evaluation justified it.

151
00:11:32,666 --> 00:11:37,833
The team's release script uses git push
--follow-tags, so the annotated release tag

152
00:11:37,873 --> 00:11:43,040
goes with the branch and the engineers'
private lightweight tags, for bisecting and

153
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:47,933
for marking a canary, stay on their
machines. Nobody in the team runs git push

154
00:11:47,973 --> 00:11:48,372
--tags.

155
00:11:50,212 --> 00:11:57,764
Your turn. Do Lab 13.1, "Three kinds of
tag", in lab-manual/m13-tags-versions.md.

156
00:11:58,104 --> 00:12:02,447
Before you create each tag, predict how
many objects the repository will have

157
00:12:02,487 --> 00:12:07,058
afterwards, what the ref file will contain,
and what git cat-file -t will say for

158
00:12:07,098 --> 00:12:11,783
the tag name. Before you peel, predict which
of the five revision expressions print

159
00:12:11,823 --> 00:12:12,466
the same ID.

160
00:12:12,806 --> 00:12:19,220
The challenge is Exercise 13.5, Level
2, command prediction, "What a tag name

161
00:12:19,260 --> 00:12:24,333
resolves to", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

162
00:12:26,173 --> 00:12:28,104
Answer out loud. I'll wait.

163
00:12:32,944 --> 00:12:37,989
A strong answer counts: how many refs and
how many new objects each command writes,

164
00:12:38,029 --> 00:12:42,951
and what each ref holds. It names the
fields of the tag object and says where the

165
00:12:42,991 --> 00:12:47,974
signature of a signed tag lives. It defines
peeling in one sentence and applies it

166
00:12:48,014 --> 00:12:52,201
to all three cases, including the
one where there is nothing to peel.

167
00:12:52,241 --> 00:12:57,163
And it adds one consequence that shows
understanding, for example what you see in

168
00:12:57,203 --> 00:13:02,248
git ls-remote for each kind, or why one
kind is ignored by git describe by default.

169
00:13:04,088 --> 00:13:07,015
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

170
00:13:07,355 --> 00:13:12,343
A lightweight tag is a ref to a commit;
an annotated tag is a ref to a tag object

171
00:13:12,383 --> 00:13:17,122
that names the commit, the tagger, the
date and a message; a signed tag is an

172
00:13:17,162 --> 00:13:19,046
annotated tag with a signature.

173
00:13:19,386 --> 00:13:23,844
^{} peels a tag to the
object it finally names.

174
00:13:24,184 --> 00:13:30,414
--sort=version:refname with versionsort.suffix
lists versions in the right order,

175
00:13:30,454 --> 00:13:34,749
and --contains tells me which
releases include a commit.

176
00:13:35,089 --> 00:13:39,812
A push sends tags only when asked; a
fetch brings tags that point into fetched

177
00:13:39,852 --> 00:13:42,071
history and never updates one I have.

178
00:13:42,411 --> 00:13:46,541
A tag lives in three places, and
deleting it in one leaves the others.

179
00:13:48,381 --> 00:13:55,745
Read sections 14B.8 to 14B.10 of Chapter
14B. Do Exercises 13.1 to 13.3, Level 1,

180
00:13:55,785 --> 00:14:02,784
"Two kinds of tag, counted in objects",
"Listing and sorting" and "Which tags

181
00:14:02,824 --> 00:14:07,902
travel", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

182
00:14:08,242 --> 00:14:13,096
Today you took a tag apart: one ref,
sometimes one object, and three places it

183
00:14:13,136 --> 00:14:17,801
lives. Create a lightweight and an
annotated tag in the lab, and peel both.

184
00:14:17,841 --> 00:14:22,883
Next time: why a published tag must not
move. Until then, look at the state first

185
00:14:22,923 --> 00:14:25,456
and type second. See you in the next one.
